meta {
  name: Resolve Address
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/address/resolve
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  ~X-Branch-Id: {{branchId}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "placeId": "{{addressPlaceId}}",
    "sessionToken": "{{addressSessionToken}}"
  }
}

tests {
  test("answers 200, or 400 for a place id Google does not recognise", function () {
    // Two statuses by design. With no GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY (the usual dev
    // machine) `Address Autocomplete` captures no place id and hands over a
    // sentinel; the service short-circuits before calling Google and answers
    // 200 { available: false }. With a key, the captured id is real and this is
    // a 200 carrying an address. A 400 means a key IS configured but the id was
    // stale or minted by a different GCP project.
    expect([200, 400]).to.include(res.getStatus());
  });

  test("a 200 carries the { available, address } envelope", function () {
    if (res.getStatus() !== 200) return;
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body).to.have.property("available");
    expect(body.available).to.be.a("boolean");
    expect(body).to.have.property("address");
  });

  test("a resolved address carries exactly the four form fields", function () {
    const body = res.getStatus() === 200 ? res.getBody() : null;
    if (!body || !body.address) return;
    expect(Object.keys(body.address).sort()).to.eql(["city", "state", "street", "zip"]);
  });

  test("state is spelled out, never the two-letter code", function () {
    const body = res.getStatus() === 200 ? res.getBody() : null;
    if (!body || !body.address || !body.address.state) return;
    // The PAC-60 acceptance criterion. Migrated SmartSuite households store
    // `Oklahoma`, so a two-letter state would sort new records apart from old
    // ones in exactly the support lookup this serves.
    expect(body.address.state.length).to.be.above(2);
  });

  test("zip is five digits — no +4", function () {
    const body = res.getStatus() === 200 ? res.getBody() : null;
    if (!body || !body.address || !body.address.zip) return;
    // ZIP is half of the household dedupe key, so a `73013-1234` would never
    // match a migrated `73013`.
    expect(body.address.zip).to.not.include("-");
  });

  test("never leaks the API key or Google's raw payload", function () {
    const raw = JSON.stringify(res.getBody());
    expect(raw).to.not.include("AIza");
    expect(raw).to.not.include("addressComponents");
    expect(raw).to.not.include("googleapis.com");
  });
}

docs {
  # POST /address/resolve — turn a chosen prediction into four fields (PAC-60)

  The second half of an address-entry session: the user clicked a suggestion
  from `POST /address/autocomplete`, and this returns the
  `{ street, city, state, zip }` the form actually holds.

  - **Module / Permission / Scope:** identical to `POST /address/autocomplete` —
    an OR over `leads`, `quote_recaps`, `deal_audits`, `crm_service` at `:write`,
    no data scope. See that request's docs for why.

  ## Request
  ```json
  {
    "placeId": "ChIJ...",
    "sessionToken": "3519edfe-0f75-4a30-bfe4-7cbd89340b2c"
  }
  ```

  `sessionToken` **must be the same value the autocomplete keystrokes carried**.
  That is what bundles them into one billed session; a mismatched or missing
  token gets every keystroke billed standalone.

  ## Response `200`
  ```json
  {
    "available": true,
    "address": {
      "street": "4821 North Maple Avenue",
      "city": "Oklahoma City",
      "state": "Oklahoma",
      "zip": "73013"
    }
  }
  ```

  `address` is `null` when the place carried no usable components — the client
  then leaves what the user typed alone rather than blanking four fields.

  ## Two mapping rules that are load-bearing, not cosmetic

  **1. `state` is the spelled-out name.** Google returns
  `administrative_area_level_1` as both `shortText: "OK"` and
  `longText: "Oklahoma"`, and **Google's own sample form maps `shortText`**.
  Copying that would split our data down the middle: `DEFAULT_ADDRESS_STATE` is
  `'Oklahoma'` and every migrated SmartSuite household stores the full name, so
  two-letter states would sort new records apart from old ones in exactly the
  support lookup this serves. Both mappers pass state through one shared
  `US_STATE_NAMES` table, which also covers the Address Validation API — where
  `postalAddress.administrativeArea` is *only* ever the short form.

  **2. `zip` is five digits — `postal_code_suffix` is deliberately dropped.**
  ZIP is half of `buildAddressKey(street, zip)`, the household dedupe key. A new
  lead keyed `73013-1234` can never match a migrated household keyed `73013`, so
  appending +4 would silently disable dedupe for every address Google happens to
  know the suffix for. Asserted in
  `packages/shared/src/domain/google-address.spec.ts` so nobody "improves" it.

  A blank `state` is backfilled with the agency default so an autofilled address
  never comes back *less* complete than the form was seeded.

  ## Nothing here rewrites stored data
  The only writer is the user clicking a suggestion, which goes through the
  form's normal field-set path. `households.propertyAddress` already coerces
  three writer key-sets on read; a silently-canonicalised fourth shape would
  make that coercion wrong with no failing test. The validation half of PAC-60
  (a "we found a slightly different address" notice) follows the same rule:
  warn and suggest, never rewrite.

  ## ⚠ Two accepted statuses, by design
  With **no `GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY`** — the usual dev machine — `Address
  Autocomplete` captures no place id and hands over the sentinel
  `no-key-configured`. The service short-circuits on `configured` before calling
  Google, so the answer is `200 { "available": false, "address": null }`. That is
  a pass, and it deliberately exercises the service rather than the validation
  pipe — an empty `placeId` would 400 at the pipe and never prove the fail-open
  path works. A `400` here means a key *is* configured but the id was stale or
  minted by a different GCP project. The `tests` block accepts both and only
  asserts the address shape when one actually came back, so the collection stays
  green with or without a key.

  ## Rate limiting
  `120 requests / minute / IP` (`ADDRESS_LOOKUP_RATE_LIMIT`), shared with
  `/address/autocomplete`. ⚠ Real environment variable only — see that request.

  ## Errors
  - `400 Bad Request` — blank/oversized `placeId` or `sessionToken`, or a place
    id Google rejects.
  - `401 Unauthorized` — missing/expired access token.
  - `403 Forbidden` — none of the four modules enabled, or none of the four write
    permissions held.
  - `429 Too Many Requests` — rate limit exceeded.

  ## Run order
  After `Address Autocomplete`, which sets both `addressSessionToken` and
  `addressPlaceId`.
}
